<section>
<h2>The challenges of archival storage</h2>
<p>Many archives preserve their scanned films as TIFF or DPX image sequences. These are grouped as multiple film reels, or individual reels in folders that are often TAR or ZIP wrapped before being copied to longterm storage. Since the beginning of digital moving image preservation it has been the dream of many archives to have a single file format that is suitable for the preservation of both film and video.</p>
<p>For further context:</p>
<ul>
<li>Reto Kromer: «<a href="https://retokromer.ch/publications/JFP_96.html" target="_blank">Matroska and FFV1: One File Format for Film and Video Archiving?</a>», in <i>Journal of Film Preservation</i>, n. 96 (April 2017)</li>
<li>Kieran O’Leary: «<a href="https://kieranjol.wordpress.com/2016/10/07/introduction-to-ffv1-and-matroska-for-film-scans/" target="_blank">Introduction to FFV1 and Matroska for Film Scans</a>», 2016–10–07</li>
</ul>
<h2>The missing piece of software</h2>
<p>The solution MediaArea have created to meet this need is <i>RAWcooked</i>. This software encodes from, and decodes to, «raw» audio-visual image sequences in a revolutionary and transparent way. The image and/or sound content, including all the embedded metadata and sidecar files, are fully preserved at the bit level.<br>
An otherwise unviewable image sequence can be viewed easily with VLC or MPV when encoded as a Matroska file. Plus this process is fully reversible returning your image sequence back to its original package content.</p>
<h2>Matroska file complete reversibility</h2>
<p>Whenever necessary, <i>RAWcooked</i> can decode the Matroska file back to the original RAW image sequence, including restoration of the original metadata and sidecar files. It is important to stress that the encoded files can be fully decoded, and that this process creates files which are bit-by-bit identical to the originals. Not only is the image and/or sound content fully preserved, but also all enclosed metadata and all of the file’s characteristics. Therefore, an encoded and decoded RAW file cannot be differentiated from its original.</p>
<h2>A complete ecosystem</h2>
<p>The Matroska files that have been encoded by <i>RAWcooked</i> can be used with many popular open source softwares which support FFV1 and FLAC playback, including:</p>
<ul>
<li><a href="https://www.videolan.org/vlc/">VLC</a> media player for quick and simple review</li>
<li><a href="https://www.ffmpeg.org/">FFmpeg</a> for transcoding, playback, metadata viewing and extraction</li>
<li><a href="{{ path('qc_home') }}">QCTools</a> advanced quality control software for audio-visual files</li>
<li><a href="{{ path('mi_home') }}">MediaInfo</a> display and export technical metadata</li>
<li><a href="{{ path('mc_home') }}">MediaConch</a> for checking the conformance of a file</li>
</ul>
<p>Being open source, they’re low cost (and free for the many commonly used flavors) allowing archives with limited resources to process the files encoded by <i>RAWcooked</i> on any computer, such as for cataloging or access purposes.</p>
</section>

<section>
<h2 id="status">Status</h2>
<p>RAWcooked is ready for production. As with any other tool, keep doing basic checks, e.g. testing reversibility with your own files.</p>
<dl class="commands">
    <dt><code>rawcooked --all /path/to/sequence/</code></dt>
    <dd>Encodes the image sequence and the audio files of the folder into <code>sequence.mkv</code>, stores the hashes of the source files in it and checks that the Matroska file can be decoded back to the source files. The source files are left intact.</dd>
    <dt><code>rawcooked sequence.mkv</code></dt>
    <dd>Decodes the Matroska file back to the original files (image sequence, audio files, sidecar files), bit-by-bit, and checks them against the stored hashes.</dd>
    <dt><code>rawcooked --check sequence.mkv</code></dt>
    <dd>Checks that the Matroska file would be decoded to the original files, without writing them.</dd>
</dl>
<p>More in the <a href="{{ docUrl }}Doc/User_Manual.md">user manual</a>, in the <a href="{{ path('rawcooked_man') }}">man page</a> (<code>rawcooked --help</code>) and in the <a href="{{ docUrl }}Doc/Case_study.md">case study of the BFI National Archive</a>.</p>

<h2 id="ffmpeg">FFmpeg</h2>
<p>RAWcooked needs FFmpeg for the encoding. Any FFmpeg with the FFV1 and FLAC encoders may be used if <code>ffmpeg</code> is in the PATH (e.g. the FFmpeg of your Linux distribution or of Homebrew), and the <code>-b</code> option selects another FFmpeg binary.</p>
<p>{{ rc.newMark(new) }} We recommend the <a href="{{ ffmpegUrl }}">FFmpeg builds of MediaArea</a>: they have GPU support and RAWcooked finds them automatically. Their libraries are also used by RAWcooked for decoding with the GPU.</p>
<ul class="flavors-notes">
<li><b>Windows</b>: create a <code>ffmpeg</code> directory beside <code>rawcooked.exe</code> and put the content of <a href="{{ ffmpegUrl }}FFmpeg_Bin_Latest_Windows_Shared_x64.zip">FFmpeg_Bin_Latest_Windows_Shared_x64.zip</a> in it (<code>ffmpeg.exe</code> is then in <code>ffmpeg\bin</code>). The Static package has no library, so no decoding with the GPU. The GPU support uses the Vulkan driver installed with the driver of the graphics card (NVIDIA, AMD or Intel).</li>
<li><b>macOS</b>: create a <code>ffmpeg</code> directory beside <code>rawcooked</code> and put the content of <a href="{{ ffmpegUrl }}FFmpeg_Bin_Latest_Mac_Shared_universal.zip">FFmpeg_Bin_Latest_Mac_Shared_universal.zip</a> in it (<code>ffmpeg</code> is then in <code>ffmpeg/bin</code>), or run the installer of <a href="{{ ffmpegUrl }}ffmpeg-ma_Latest_Mac.dmg">ffmpeg-ma_Latest_Mac.dmg</a>. The GPU support needs a Mac with Apple silicon and macOS 26 or later, the Vulkan driver is in both packages.</li>
<li><b>Linux</b>: install the <code>ffmpeg-ma</code> package of your distribution from <a href="{{ ffmpegUrl }}">the list of builds</a>, it does not replace the FFmpeg of the distribution. The GPU support uses the Vulkan driver of the graphics card (driver of NVIDIA, or Mesa for AMD and Intel).</li>
</ul>
<p>More details are in the <a href="https://github.com/MediaArea/RAWcooked#ffmpeg">README</a>, and <code>--show-stats</code> shows if the GPU is used.</p>
</section>

<section>
<h2 id="input">Supported input formats</h2>
<p><span class="supported-default">D = Supported by default</span>
<span class="supported-key">K = Supported with a key, please <a href="{{ path('ma_contact') }}">contact us</a></span>
<span class="supported-needfile">N = Need file &amp; development if interest, please <a href="{{ path('ma_contact') }}">contact us</a></span></p>
<p>A key for a flavor is needed only for encoding: decoding a file back to the original files and checking it never need a key.</p>

<div class="row">
<div class="col-md-6">
<h3 id="dpx">DPX</h3>
<p>Uncompressed, 1 image element, unsigned samples, left to right and top to bottom.</p>
<div class="table-responsive">
<table class="download table table-bordered table-condensed flavors">
  <tr class="table-header">
    <th>Bit depth</th>
    <th>Packing</th>
    <th>Endian&shy;ness</th>
    <th>Y</th>
    <th>RGB</th>
    <th>RGBA</th>
  </tr>
  <tr>
    <td>8-bit</td>
    <td></td>
    <td></td>
    {{ rc.cell('K') }}
    {{ rc.cell('D') }}
    {{ rc.cell('K') }}
  </tr>
  <tr>
    <td rowspan="4">10-bit</td>
    <td rowspan="2">Filled A</td>
    <td><abbr title="Little endian">LE</abbr></td>
    {{ rc.cell('N') }}
    {{ rc.cell('D') }}
    {{ rc.cell('K') }}
  </tr>
  <tr>
    <td><abbr title="Big endian">BE</abbr></td>
    {{ rc.cell('K') }}
    {{ rc.cell('D') }}
    {{ rc.cell('K') }}
  </tr>
  <tr>
    <td rowspan="2">Filled B</td>
    <td><abbr title="Little endian">LE</abbr></td>
    {{ rc.cell('N') }}
    {{ rc.cell('N') }}
    {{ rc.cell('N') }}
  </tr>
  <tr>
    <td><abbr title="Big endian">BE</abbr></td>
    {{ rc.cell('K') }}
    {{ rc.cell('N') }}
    {{ rc.cell('N') }}
  </tr>
  <tr>
    <td rowspan="4">12-bit</td>
    <td rowspan="2">Packed</td>
    <td><abbr title="Little endian">LE</abbr></td>
    {{ rc.cell('N') }}
    {{ rc.cell('N') }}
    {{ rc.cell('N') }}
  </tr>
  <tr>
    <td><abbr title="Big endian">BE</abbr></td>
    {{ rc.cell('K') }}
    {{ rc.cell('K') }}
    {{ rc.cell('K') }}
  </tr>
  <tr>
    <td rowspan="2">Filled A</td>
    <td><abbr title="Little endian">LE</abbr></td>
    {{ rc.cell('N') }}
    {{ rc.cell('K') }}
    {{ rc.cell('K') }}
  </tr>
  <tr>
    <td><abbr title="Big endian">BE</abbr></td>
    {{ rc.cell('N') }}
    {{ rc.cell('K') }}
    {{ rc.cell('K') }}
  </tr>
  <tr>
    <td rowspan="2">16-bit</td>
    <td rowspan="2"></td>
    <td><abbr title="Little endian">LE</abbr></td>
    {{ rc.cell('K') }}
    {{ rc.cell('K') }}
    {{ rc.cell('K') }}
  </tr>
  <tr>
    <td><abbr title="Big endian">BE</abbr></td>
    {{ rc.cell('K') }}
    {{ rc.cell('K') }}
    {{ rc.cell('K') }}
  </tr>
  <tr>
    <td>16-bit float</td>
    <td></td>
    <td></td>
    {{ rc.cell('N') }}
    {{ rc.cell('N') }}
    {{ rc.cell('N') }}
  </tr>
</table>
</div>
<ul class="flavors-notes">
<li>8-bit: files of both byte orders.</li>
<li>12-bit packed big endian (RGB, Y): also bottom to top.</li>
<li>Y 10-bit: also the files without padding at the end of the lines of Lasergraphics scanners (Director included {{ rc.newMark(new) }}), DIAMANT-Film and Scanity.</li>
<li>Padding bits which are not zero are kept (<code>--check-padding</code>).</li>
<li>Some common non conformities are accepted, e.g. a version number in lowercase or empty, the packing value 3 with Y 16-bit.</li>
</ul>
</div>

<div class="col-md-6">
<h3 id="tiff">TIFF</h3>
<p>Uncompressed, strips stored one after the other, unsigned integer samples, top left orientation.</p>
<div class="table-responsive">
<table class="download table table-bordered table-condensed flavors">
  <tr class="table-header">
    <th rowspan="2">Bits per sample</th>
    <th rowspan="2">Y</th>
    <th colspan="2">RGB</th>
    <th rowspan="2">RGBA</th>
  </tr>
  <tr class="table-header">
    <th>Chunky</th>
    <th>Planar</th>
  </tr>
  {% for bits in ['2-bit', '4-bit', '6-bit'] %}
  <tr>
    <td>{{ bits }}</td>
    {{ rc.cell('K', new) }}
    {{ rc.cell('K', new) }}
    {{ rc.cell('K', new) }}
    {{ rc.cell('N') }}
  </tr>
  {% endfor %}
  <tr>
    <td>8-bit</td>
    {{ rc.cell('K') }}
    {{ rc.cell('K') }}
    {{ rc.cell('K', new) }}
    {{ rc.cell('K') }}
  </tr>
  {% for bits in ['10-bit', '12-bit', '14-bit'] %}
  <tr>
    <td>{{ bits }}</td>
    {{ rc.cell('K', new) }}
    {{ rc.cell('K', new) }}
    {{ rc.cell('K', new) }}
    {{ rc.cell('N') }}
  </tr>
  {% endfor %}
  <tr>
    <td>16-bit <abbr title="Little endian">LE</abbr></td>
    {{ rc.cell('K') }}
    {{ rc.cell('K') }}
    {{ rc.cell('K', new) }}
    {{ rc.cell('K') }}
  </tr>
  <tr>
    <td>16-bit <abbr title="Big endian">BE</abbr></td>
    {{ rc.cell('K') }}
    {{ rc.cell('K') }}
    {{ rc.cell('K', new) }}
    {{ rc.cell('N') }}
  </tr>
</table>
</div>
<ul class="flavors-notes">
<li>2 to 14-bit: files of both byte orders (II and MM); the samples of 2, 4, 6, 10, 12 and 14 bits are packed as in the TIFF specification, the padding bits at the end of the lines are kept if they are not zero.</li>
<li>RGBA: unassociated alpha.</li>
<li>XMP, IPTC, Photoshop, Exif, GPS, ICC profile and PrintIM tags are accepted {{ rc.newMark(new) }}.</li>
<li>The new flavors need a recent FFmpeg, and <code>--check</code> is always done for them.</li>
</ul>

</div>
</div>

<div class="row">
<div class="col-md-6">
<h3 id="exr">EXR</h3>
<p>Uncompressed scan lines in increasing Y order, single part, data window same as the display window.</p>
<div class="table-responsive">
<table class="download table table-bordered table-condensed flavors">
  <tr class="table-header">
    <th>Components</th>
    <th>Pixel type</th>
    <th style="width: 20%">Support</th>
  </tr>
  <tr>
    <td rowspan="3">RGB</td>
    <td>16-bit float (half)</td>
    {{ rc.cell('K') }}
  </tr>
  <tr>
    <td>32-bit float</td>
    {{ rc.cell('N') }}
  </tr>
  <tr>
    <td>32-bit unsigned integer</td>
    {{ rc.cell('N') }}
  </tr>
</table>
</div>
<ul class="flavors-notes">
<li>Common attributes and the attributes of ARRI are accepted, as well as the attributes of FilmLight and DPX metadata {{ rc.newMark(new) }}.</li>
<li>The support of EXR depends on the FFmpeg version, <code>--check</code> is required.</li>
</ul>

</div>

<div class="col-md-6">
<h3 id="avi">AVI</h3>
<div class="table-responsive">
<table class="download table table-bordered table-condensed flavors">
  <tr class="table-header">
    <th>Video</th>
    <th>Audio</th>
    <th style="width: 20%">Support</th>
  </tr>
  <tr>
    <td>v210 (YUV 4:2:2 10-bit)</td>
    <td>None, or PCM (same flavors as WAV)</td>
    {{ rc.cell('K') }}
  </tr>
</table>
</div>
<ul class="flavors-notes">
<li>1 video track, then 1 audio track at most; width multiple of 48 pixels.</li>
<li><code>--check</code> is always done.</li>
</ul>
</div>
</div>

<h3 id="audio">Audio</h3>
<div class="table-responsive">
<table class="download table table-bordered table-condensed flavors">
  <tr class="table-header">
    <th>PCM in</th>
    <th>Sampling rate</th>
    <th>Bit depth</th>
    <th>Channels</th>
    <th>Support</th>
  </tr>
  <tr>
    <td rowspan="2">WAV, BWF, RF64</td>
    <td>48 kHz</td>
    <td>16-bit</td>
    <td>2</td>
    {{ rc.cell('D') }}
  </tr>
  <tr>
    <td>44.1, 48, 96 kHz</td>
    <td>8, 16, 24, 32-bit, 32-bit float</td>
    <td>1, 2, 4, 6, 8</td>
    {{ rc.cell('K') }}
  </tr>
  <tr>
    <td>AIFF, AIFF-C</td>
    <td>44.1, 48, 96 kHz</td>
    <td>8, 16, 24, 32-bit</td>
    <td>1, 2, 4, 6, 8</td>
    {{ rc.cell('K') }}
  </tr>
  <tr>
    <td colspan="4">Other sampling rates, other counts of channels</td>
    {{ rc.cell('N') }}
  </tr>
</table>
</div>
<ul class="flavors-notes">
<li>AIFF: big endian, and little endian (<code>sowt</code>) with 16-bit; 8-bit signed or unsigned.</li>
<li>32-bit audio is stored without compression in the Matroska file.</li>
</ul>

<p>Another format/flavor you want us to support (e.g. little endian…)? <a href="{{ path('ma_contact') }}">Contact us</a>.</p>

